Security model
A private website is not secure by default. Every layer below must be built and tested.
Owner authentication
• Firebase Authentication with one owner identity. No public signup, anonymous access, or provider that accepts arbitrary accounts.
• Multi-factor authentication on the owner account.
• Owner identity checked server-side on every privileged request, not only in the browser.
Firestore rules
• Deny by default. Every collection needs an explicit rule.
• Rules check owner identity, data types, required fields, and mode field.
• The browser cannot write trade, decision, log, or telemetry records. It may write only validated settings requests.
• Rules tested in the Firebase Emulator for read, write, update, and delete.
Backend authorization and IAM
• Admin SDK bypasses client rules. Backend operations are protected by service-account IAM with least privilege, and by backend checks on each request.
• No service-account key files in the repository or the browser.
Authenticated telemetry
• EA messages carry a per-message signature or HMAC, a nonce, and a timestamp.
• Timestamps outside an approved tolerance are rejected. Replayed nonces are rejected.
• Each telemetry field has its own freshness limit.
• A Firestore record alone never proves the EA is connected.
Secrets
• Secrets live in environment or secret storage, never in code, the dashboard bundle, or logs.
• Owner chat and documents must not receive passwords, tokens, service-account JSON, or broker credentials.
Settings safety
• Safety-critical settings (risk limits, mode, symbol) are validated, versioned, logged, and applied by the EA only after its own checks.
Rate limiting, input validation, and dependency scanning apply to every backend endpoint.
Dashboard specification
The dashboard is the primary interface. It is owner-only, responsive, and built to work on a phone and on a desktop. Every panel shows its data mode (LIVE, DEMO, HISTORICAL, SAMPLE, UNVERIFIED, or NOT VERIFIED) and the freshness of each field.
