Supporting system engines
These are system services, not Brains. Each has a defined input, output, and failure state.
• Session Intelligence: Asian, London, and New York sessions, overlap, and broker server time with daylight-saving handling. Input: broker time and symbol session data. Failure: unknown session time disables session-dependent logic.
• Volatility Service: ATR, candle range, expansion and contraction, unusual movement. Output: volatility state and a restriction flag. Failure: missing ATR history blocks volatility-dependent candidates.
• News and Event Risk Service: high-impact events with timestamps and a verified source. Output: event window and trading restriction. Failure: unverified or missing calendar data sets the state to NOT VERIFIED and restricts trading.
• Strategy Engine: loads only approved, versioned strategy specifications. Unapproved rules cannot enter production.
• Entry Quality Engine: checks objective entry conditions for a candidate.
• Execution Quality Engine: spread, slippage, requotes, rejected orders, latency, and request status. Runs in the EA.
• Trade Management Engine: stop-loss, take-profit, break-even, trailing stop, partial close, and emergency exit, each only under approved rules. Runs in the EA.
• Logging and State Engine: decisions, Brain outputs, risk approvals and rejections, order requests, broker results, position state, errors, restart recovery, and audit history.
Open decision: whether Session and Volatility run in the backend, the EA, or both. The EA must hold the final copy of any value it uses to decide an order.
Strategy research and testing
Three candidate families are compared under identical conditions. None is pre-approved.
• Strategy A, trend-aligned pullback: higher-timeframe direction, defined pullback, confirmation, invalidation, stop, target, regime suitability.
• Strategy B, breakout and retest: defined breakout, closing confirmation, retest, failed-breakout rules, trigger, invalidation, stop, target.
• Strategy C, liquidity sweep and reversal: liquidity level, sweep definition, reclaim, structure and displacement confirmation, trigger, invalidation, stop, target.
Fair testing protocol
• Same data quality requirements, date ranges, spread, commission, swap, and slippage assumptions.
• Real-tick backtesting where the data is available, with the data source recorded.
• Out-of-sample and walk-forward validation, split across market regimes.
• Drawdown, sample size, parameter sensitivity, and overfitting checks.
• Risk-adjusted metrics. Net profit alone never selects a strategy.
Validation sequence
1.  Historical backtest.
2.  Out-of-sample test.
3.  Walk-forward test.
4.  Robustness test.
5.  Demo forward evaluation of about 8 to 12 weeks.
No strategy is declared profitable from one backtest. Results appear in the Strategy Lab with source, date range, cost assumptions, and sample size. Strategy selection requires evidence and Founder approval.
Risk and Safety Supervisor
The Supervisor lives inside the MT5 EA. It is independent of the backend and the Central Decision Engine. It can refuse any candidate, even one every Brain supports. The backend cannot disable it.
Confirmed limits
• Risk per trade: 0.50%.
• Daily loss limit: 2%.
• Weekly loss limit: 5%.
• Three consecutive losses: 180-minute pause.
• Maximum open positions: 1.
• No martingale, grid, averaging up, or averaging down.
Supervisor checks
• Per-trade risk and position size against the account and broker specifications.
• Stop-loss exists, is valid for the broker, and is on the correct side of price.
• Spread and execution conditions within limits.
• Duplicate-order protection using request IDs.
• Restart recovery of position and pause state.
• Connection and telemetry failure: fail closed.
• Emergency suspension by the owner, from the dashboard, applied by the EA.
Dashboard role: read-only display of Supervisor state. Limit changes are proposed in Personal Settings, validated by the backend, and applied by the EA only after its own checks, with an audit entry.
