Architecture options compared
• Option A, MT5-centric: simplest execution path; weak for dashboards, history, and mobile access.
• Option B, hybrid: MT5 for execution and final safety, backend for intelligence, dashboard on Firebase. Recommended, because it keeps the safety path near the broker and separates analysis from control.
• Option C, backend-centric: strong analysis and testing; larger trust boundary and more latency between backend and broker.
Hosting for the backend and dashboard should be chosen after the architecture is approved. Firebase Hosting, Cloud Functions, and Storage are optional and need a stated reason before use.
Development roadmap, dependencies and acceptance criteria
Phase gates: nothing starts until the previous phase has passed its audit and the Founder has approved it.
Phase 1: Foundation and infrastructure
• Scope: final architecture, project structure, TradingView foundation V1, MT5 EA foundation, backend skeleton, owner authentication, dashboard shell, data schemas, communication contracts, DEMO and LIVE data model, logging, error handling, Firestore rules, test infrastructure, CI, documentation.
• No BUY or SELL signals, no order placement, no real-money trading.
• Acceptance: TradingView script compiles and passes its tests; EA compiles and reads broker specs in a real terminal; rules tests pass in the emulator; authentication denies non-owners; telemetry rejects replays, bad timestamps, and invalid prices; dashboard renders every state including unavailable and error.
Phase 2: Twelve Brains, intelligence, and strategy research
• Scope: specifications and implementations of Brains 01 to 12, supporting engines, Central Decision Engine, independent Supervisor logic, decision logging, strategies A, B, and C, backtests, out-of-sample and walk-forward tests, robustness and overfitting analysis.
• No live order execution.
• Acceptance: each Brain has spec, schemas, tests, and documented limitations; no fabricated output; strategy results reproducible with recorded data sources.
Phase 3: MT5 execution, trade management, and validation
• Scope: order requests, broker response validation, position identification, stop-loss and take-profit management, sizing, break-even, trailing, partial close, emergency exit, duplicate protection, restart recovery, reconciliation, verified trade history, analytics, monitoring.
• Demo forward evaluation of about 8 to 12 weeks before any live decision.
• Acceptance: execution confirmed from broker results only; reconciliation matches broker history; recovery tested by forced restarts and network loss.
